WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.940
U.S. corn subsidies meant to steady farm incomes instead flooded the food supply with cheap

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:11.360
sweetener, feedlot beef, and ethanol. This is unintended consequences, episode 76.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.960
In the spring of 1984, executives at Coca-Cola quietly switched the formula for its flagship

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:24.240
drink from cane sugar to high-fructose corn syrup. The change saved the company millions and

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.980
spread within months to Pepsi and most other major brands. What began as a new deal at

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.920
effort to keep corn farmers solvent had, by then produced a river of low-cost corn that no

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:42.140
one in 1933 had set out to create. The policy took shape during the Great Depression and

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:47.860
the Dust Bowl. Franklin Roosevelt's Secretary of Agriculture Henry Wallace watched farm prices

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:54.700
collapse and rural banks fail. The Agricultural Adjustment Act of 1933 and the Commodity

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:00.480
Credit Corporation that followed offered non-recourse loans and price supports, so growers

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:07.080
would not have to sell at harvest time lows. The explicit goal was income stability and a reliable

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:13.940
domestic food supply, not abundance for its own sake. Wallace and his successors saw surplus as the

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:20.120
lesser danger after years of scarcity and foreclosure. The approach treated corn as a strategic

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:26.020
reserve crop whose steady production would buffer the wider economy. At the time, the arithmetic

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.800
was straightforward. If loan rates covered production costs and allowed farmers to hold grain off

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.540
the market until prices recovered, the cycle of foreclosure and rural bank runs would slow.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:43.100
Wallace's team understood that corn could be stored more easily than many other crops,

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.140
making it a practical anchor for the entire price support system.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:54.720
Through the 1950s and 1960s, price support loans and acreage allotments kept corn prices above

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:02.660
the cost of production in most years. The 1972 Russian grain sale and the 1973 farm bill shifted

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:09.060
the emphasis. Agriculture Secretary Earl Butts replaced tight supply controls with direct payments

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:15.280
that rewarded higher yields. Target prices and deficiency payments guaranteed farmers a return

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:22.700
even when market prices fell. By the late 1970s, corn yields per acre had risen sharply and total

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:28.540
production climbed. Early evaluations from the Department of Agriculture described the system as

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:35.160
successful at raising farm receipts and expanding exports. The new payments worked by calculating the

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:40.900
gap between a legislated target price and the lower market price. The government covered the

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:46.940
difference on a per bushel basis for eligible production. This removed the earlier incentive to

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:52.560
limit acreage because farmers could now plant more and still receive the deficiency payment on every

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:59.280
qualifying bushel. The same price signals that encouraged more corn also drove its real market price

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:06.380
downward once output exceeded traditional uses. Wet milling capacity expanded in the Midwest and by

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:13.580
1980, high fructose corn syrup had become cheaper than imported sugar. Soft drink makers completed

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:20.820
the switch by 1985. Estimates suggest high fructose corn syrup captured roughly 40% of the US

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:27.200
sweetener market within a decade. Livestock feeders discovered that corn could be finished in confinement

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.840
at lower cost than grass or smaller grains. Accelerating the growth of large feed lots in Kansas,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:41.200
Nebraska and Texas. The 2005 and 2007 energy acts added a new guaranteed buyer.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:48.600
Ethanol refineries whose mandates absorb roughly 40% of the annual corn crop by the mid 2010s.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.760
Each downstream sector treated corn as an abundant policy stabilized input rather than a scarce

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:00.540
commodity. Because the subsidy attached to the bushel rather than to any particular end use,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:06.040
processors and integrators captured much of the value while the original income support rationale

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:12.440
remained intact. Public health researchers have documented rising per capita intake of caloric

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:18.460
sweeteners and shifts in fatty acid profiles in the food supply. Though the precise contribution

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:22.700
of these changes to population weight trends remains contested in the literature.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:29.460
The causal chain ran from loan rates and deficiency payments to lower relative prices to capital

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.960
investment in milling, feeding and distillation infrastructure that proved difficult to reverse.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:42.020
Wet milling itself illustrates the mechanism. The process converts starch into glucose and then

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:48.720
isomerizes part of it into fructose, requiring large-scale capital that only becomes economic when

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.760
corn is reliably below a certain price threshold. Once those plants were built and amortized,

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.720
the industry had a continuing interest in maintaining high corn throughput.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:05.380
Feedlots followed a parallel logic. Operators could buy corn below the cost of growing grass or

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:11.460
buying hay, then market finished cattle at prices that reflected the subsidized grain rather than

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:18.200
pasture costs. Ethanol mandates later locked in another fixed demand curve because blenders faced

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:24.660
legal requirements to use set volumes regardless of corn price swings. Congress has revisited

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:30.860
the structure in every farm bill since 1985, trimming target prices, introducing crop insurance

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:37.880
subsidies and adding conservation reserve provisions. Sugar import quotas and tariffs have kept domestic

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:43.000
cane and beet prices higher than corn sweetener, preserving some market separation.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:50.000
Ethanol mandates have been adjusted but not repealed. Direct payments to corn producers were largely

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.840
replaced by insurance and risk management programs in the 2014 and 2018 farm bills.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:03.680
Yet total federal support for the sector remains substantial. No subsequent legislation has attempted

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.140
to unwind the underlying yield incentives or to price corn at a level that would replicate pre-1970

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:16.660
scarcity. The system continues to deliver low-cost feedstock to the same industries that expanded

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:23.080
under the earlier regime. Insurance-based support still pays indemnities when yields or prices fall

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:29.280
below guaranteed levels, which preserves the incentive to plant more acres because the downside risk

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:36.280
is partly socialized. When public policy guarantees returns on a single input, private capital migrates

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:42.820
toward whatever can be made from that input at the new price. The original objective of stable farm

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:48.060
income can be achieved while the composition of the entire downstream economy changes without

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:54.140
legislative debate. Complex supply chains treat subsidized abundance as a permanent condition

00:06:54.140 --> 00:07:00.660
and build processing capacity accordingly. Before extending support to any commodity, it is worth

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:06.520
asking which industries will be created or enlarged by the resulting price signal and whether those

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:12.620
industries align with later public goals. Once those industries exist, reversing the original

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:18.900
support becomes politically and economically harder because the new infrastructure depends on continued

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:25.360
low input costs. Consider today's subsidies for critical minerals used in electric vehicle batteries

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:31.100
and ask which new processing and manufacturing sectors might grow around that guaranteed supply.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.660
And that's where today's story leaves us. If it changed how you see one everyday decision,

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.320
that's the whole point. Pass it along to someone who depreciated and we'll be back tomorrow with

